00:38:41,550 --> 00:38:44,550
I've used this like about six times.

430
00:38:44,550 --> 00:38:45,710
So.

431
00:38:45,710 --> 00:38:46,710
But it turns out.

432
00:38:46,710 --> 00:38:49,710
It's the simplest one to implement, so we'll go ahead and do it.

433
00:38:49,710 --> 00:38:52,710
So it's called the do while loop.

434
00:38:52,710 --> 00:38:59,710
And so the key word is do, and then there's some stuff you do, that's the body of the do.

435
00:38:59,710 --> 00:39:05,710
And then there's a test at the end that tells you whether to keep repeating this loop.

436
00:39:05,710 --> 00:39:07,710
So it sort of flips a normal while loop.

437
00:39:07,710 --> 00:39:11,710
You have the conditional test at the front, and here you have it at the end.

438
00:39:11,710 --> 00:39:18,440
And so it turns out if we think in terms of go-to, that's a pretty

439
00:39:18,440 --> 00:39:25,440
straightforward thing to replace this with a code that will do a test.

440
00:39:25,440 --> 00:39:33,440
And if the test holds, it will jump back and say, okay, re-execute that part of the program I was just in.

441
00:39:33,440 --> 00:39:43,530
So it sets up a loop where you can think of it's literally a loop of control flow going round and round.

442
00:39:43,530 --> 00:39:50,960
And by the way, this is actually a semi-useful function. It's called pop count.

443
00:39:50,960 --> 00:39:51,960
sometimes.

444
00:39:51,960 --> 00:39:56,280
How many ones are there in a word of data?

445
00:39:56,280 --> 00:39:58,280
In this case, it's 64-bit word.

446
00:39:58,280 --> 00:40:02,280
There's been some generations of data where you're

447
00:40:02,280 --> 00:40:06,280
required to write this function using all your favorite operations

448
00:40:06,280 --> 00:40:12,500
and with a fairly small limit on operations.

449
00:40:12,500 --> 00:40:16,940
But we're not doing that today.

450
00:40:16,940 --> 00:40:25,190
So here, my point is you see that there's a fairly straightforward way of

451
00:40:25,190 --> 00:40:30,190
taking this particular loop and turning it into just a combination of conditional

452
00:40:30,190 --> 00:40:34,440
that where you do is you jump back to where you were before.

453
00:40:34,440 --> 00:40:38,240
And here's the actual code for it.

454
00:40:38,240 --> 00:40:42,240
I won't go through all of it, but you can believe from these annotations

455
00:40:42,240 --> 00:40:45,240
that it very closely follows the code here.

456
00:40:45,240 --> 00:40:51,330
And the main thing to see is this jump, if not equal,

457
00:40:51,330 --> 00:40:57,190
to label L2, and that takes you back to here.

458
00:40:57,190 --> 00:40:59,190
And so the general form of it then,

459
00:40:59,190 --> 00:41:05,400
is you take a do and then this part of it's called the body of the loop and

460
00:41:05,400 --> 00:41:06,400
this is the test.

461
00:41:06,400 --> 00:41:10,400
And so now you set up some code where you can execute the body,

462
00:41:10,400 --> 00:41:14,400
perform the test, and if the test holds, you go back to the loop.

463
00:41:14,400 --> 00:41:20,420
So a while loop is really just like a do loop,

464
00:41:20,420 --> 00:41:24,420
a do while loop, except that you move the test to the beginning.

465
00:41:24,420 --> 00:41:27,420
And the only difference, therefore, is with a do loop,

466
00:41:27,420 --> 00:41:31,420
you don't do the test the first time through.

467
00:41:31,420 --> 00:41:32,420
You're guaranteed.

468
00:41:32,420 --> 00:41:35,420
to do it the first time no matter what.

469
00:41:35,420 --> 00:41:40,520
While a while loop will explicitly test even at the very beginning

470
00:41:40,520 --> 00:41:45,220
and skip over the loop if the condition doesn't hold.

471
00:41:45,220 --> 00:41:48,220
So there's two ways to generate a code.

472
00:41:48,220 --> 00:41:51,220
Well, there's more than two ways to generate code for a while loop.

473
00:41:51,220 --> 00:41:54,220
But you'll find GCC actually uses two different ways.

474
00:41:54,220 --> 00:42:00,220
One is what we'll find when we use this optimization setting I talked about

475
00:42:00,220 --> 00:42:03,200
last time called minus capital O, which stands

476
00:42:03,220 --> 00:42:08,220
for optimize G, which means debug, of course,

477
00:42:08,220 --> 00:42:10,220
as you've already know.

478
00:42:10,220 --> 00:42:14,220
There's some history to what the minus G flags means.

479
00:42:14,220 --> 00:42:17,440
So, and as I mentioned last time,

480
00:42:17,440 --> 00:42:20,440
this turns out to be the perfect level of optimization for

481
00:42:20,440 --> 00:42:23,440
this course, where you want to be able to look at machine code

482
00:42:23,440 --> 00:42:27,440
and understand it and how it relates to the C code.

483
00:42:27,440 --> 00:42:30,440
Because it does some sort of simple optimizations,

484
00:42:30,440 --> 00:42:33,420
but it doesn't try to rewrite your whole program to make it run

485
00:42:33,440 --> 00:42:39,050
better. Whereas even with minus 01, which is the next level in the optimization,

486
00:42:39,050 --> 00:42:43,050
you'll find sometimes it will do some pretty quirky stuff.

487
00:42:43,050 --> 00:42:50,400
But when you actually, as a program developer, as a software developer out there,

488
00:42:50,400 --> 00:42:54,400
or you talk to companies and you ask what optimization level do you use,

489
00:42:54,400 --> 00:42:59,400
they'll probably say minus 02. That's sort of the common one.

490
00:42:59,400 --> 00:43:05,010
So usually there's higher levels of optimization, and we're purposely backing off

491
00:43:05,010 --> 00:43:08,270
that to make this code easier to understand.

492
00:43:08,270 --> 00:43:12,270
So anyways, with this default optimization

493
00:43:12,270 --> 00:43:16,270
we're using for this course, it does a transformation I call the

494
00:43:16,270 --> 00:43:18,270
jump-to-middle translation.

495
00:43:18,270 --> 00:43:20,270
These are, by the way, my own names.

496
00:43:20,270 --> 00:43:23,270
You won't find them in any other document.

497
00:43:23,270 --> 00:43:29,300
But the idea of it is, imagine again, I have a while-loop

498
00:43:29,300 --> 00:43:34,300
of this form where there's a test and a body to execute.

499
00:43:34,300 --> 00:43:35,300
Well, what I'll, the problem.

500
00:43:35,300 --> 00:43:39,300
I have to do compared to before is somehow I have to execute the test

501
00:43:39,300 --> 00:43:42,300
before I start the first iteration of the loop.

502
00:43:42,300 --> 00:43:45,300
So the way I'll do it here is I'll just jump to the middle

503
00:43:45,300 --> 00:43:49,300
of the what was the old loop code

504
00:43:49,300 --> 00:43:52,300
to the test portion of that loop.

505
00:43:52,300 --> 00:43:55,300
And then let that test determine whether I actually

506
00:43:55,300 --> 00:43:59,520
enter the loop itself.

507
00:43:59,520 --> 00:44:05,580
And so this is an example of a go-to code

508
00:44:05,580 --> 00:44:09,580
a code version of my pop count function.

509
00:44:09,580 --> 00:44:14,640
So I've written the pop count function using a while loop this time.

510
00:44:14,640 --> 00:44:21,180
It turns out whether you use a do or a while loop, you'll get the same result in this particular function.

511
00:44:21,180 --> 00:44:30,720
And you'll see the way it looks is it jumps to the test portion of the code,

512
00:44:30,720 --> 00:44:35,720
and then if that holds it then actually starts in the first execution of the loop.

513
00:44:35,720 --> 00:44:36,720
And then from there on it looks the same.

514
00:44:36,720 --> 00:44:42,000
it looks the same as a do-while loop. It just does the what was in the

515
00:44:42,000 --> 00:44:51,290
loop, does the test, jumps back to the beginning. And then there's a second method which

516
00:44:51,290 --> 00:44:57,430
is in some ways more traditional. And you'll find that if you use optimization 1, which

517
00:44:57,430 --> 00:45:04,210
is a fairly useful thing to do in practice. So just to show you that instead of deciding

518
00:45:04,210 --> 00:45:09,940
which one I was going to show you, I decided to show you both. And the idea of that, and the idea

519
00:45:09,940 --> 00:45:14,880
of that is to do a, essentially to take a while loop and turn it into a due

520
00:45:14,880 --> 00:45:22,480
while loop, but introducing a conditional beforehand that would essentially guard your entrance

521
00:45:22,480 --> 00:45:29,940
to the loop. So you do an initial test, and if that's false, then you skip over the loop altogether,

522
00:45:29,940 --> 00:45:37,310
otherwise it's performed like a do while loop. And so that has a fairly straightforward

523
00:45:37,310 --> 00:45:42,090
translation into code. And that, again, it looks a little different from before.

524
00:45:42,110 --> 00:45:47,790
that it has an initial test, but then it looks like a do-while loop.

525
00:45:51,760 --> 00:45:56,940
So I won't show you the assembly versions of those, but if you, if you looked at it, actually,

526
00:45:56,940 --> 00:46:04,660
the code is all in directories that are linked to your, the class directory, the class directory,

527
00:46:04,660 --> 00:46:10,160
so you can actually look at the code. And you'll see that it follows exactly the way I'm

528
00:46:10,160 --> 00:46:16,230
describing it. And then a third kind of loop is the four loop.

529
00:46:16,750 --> 00:46:21,620
which in C is actually a very powerful,

530
00:46:21,620 --> 00:46:25,060
very general type of thing, and so it's very commonly used.

531
00:46:25,060 --> 00:46:30,240
But you can see that the four loop has four components to it.

532
00:46:30,240 --> 00:46:35,620
It has an initialization, it has a test, has a rule for doing an update

533
00:46:35,620 --> 00:46:40,820
in case as a way to continue the loop, and then it has the body of the loop.

534
00:46:40,820 --> 00:46:47,600
And so here's a version of my pop count function, where I'm just going through all

535
00:46:47,620 --> 00:46:55,850
the bits in the word, extracting them out, and adding them, accumulating them to be the result.

536
00:46:56,950 --> 00:47:05,850
So, ripping apart this four, you'll see that these four blocks of the loop correspond to the initialization

537
00:47:05,850 --> 00:47:16,400
is to set I equal to zero. The test is to compare I to the word size, which I've set here to be eight times.

538
00:47:16,400 --> 00:47:22,000
So however many bytes there are in an int, multiply that by 8 to get the word size.

539
00:47:23,150 --> 00:47:28,590
And then the update is i++, and then the body is this stuff that's within the brackets here.

540
00:47:30,660 --> 00:47:45,220
So I can think of that in general, one general way to think about a four loop in C is by this rule that will turn a four loop into a while loop.

541
00:47:47,260 --> 00:47:50,760
And so what it says is I first,

542
00:47:50,780 --> 00:47:57,660
do the initialization and then I do a loop where I guard it with this test.

543
00:47:57,660 --> 00:48:06,220
And then what I keep doing is executing the body and then performing whatever update there is,

544
00:48:06,220 --> 00:48:08,700
and then continuing with the test.

545
00:48:08,700 --> 00:48:19,060
So that's sort of the semantics, the definition of the behavior of a for loop in C. So I can take

546
00:48:19,060 --> 00:48:26,020
my different pieces of my for loop and say, well, okay, I'll take that rule and use

547
00:48:26,020 --> 00:48:30,140
it to pack them in to form a while loop.

548
00:48:30,140 --> 00:48:35,680
And of course, you know that you can sort of go between those two, and it's somewhat a matter of taste,

549
00:48:35,820 --> 00:48:39,600
whether a given loop should be written using a for loop or a while loop.

550
00:48:43,900 --> 00:48:52,470
So one reason why it's actually useful to do this minus 01 optimist.

551
00:48:52,490 --> 00:48:57,770
I talk to you about is it's fairly common for the initial test to be trivially

552
00:48:57,770 --> 00:49:04,710
trivially true. So for example, in here if i equals zero and I'm comparing it to the

553
00:49:04,710 --> 00:49:12,170
word size, which would typically be 32, well, that test is true, that's a certain thing. And so

554
00:49:12,170 --> 00:49:17,790
what you'll see is that the compiler can figure that out and just throw away this

555
00:49:17,790 --> 00:49:22,310
guarding code that would have to be there otherwise.

556
00:49:22,550 --> 00:49:35,540
So that, I believe, is the main reason why this is the transformation that gets used with the 01 optimization level is that oftentimes it doesn't need this initial test.

557
00:49:40,560 --> 00:49:48,940
Okay, so now I'm going to finish up with what's in, to me at least, the loop stuff is sort of what you'd expect.

558
00:49:50,420 --> 00:49:57,520
If you were to have to, you know, you know, turn C code into something very low level.

559
00:49:57,540 --> 00:50:01,160
where all you could do is jumps and tests.

560
00:50:01,160 --> 00:50:03,520
Well, maybe that's obvious, but maybe not.

561
00:50:04,120 --> 00:50:07,300
The switch statement, on the other hand, is a totally different beast.

562
00:50:07,860 --> 00:50:12,700
And I want to show you that because it has no counterpart in normal C-code

563
00:50:12,700 --> 00:50:14,720
of how you'd even think about it.

564
00:50:16,260 --> 00:50:23,100
And it's fairly a little bit harder to decipher, and you're going to have to do it in the bomb lab.

565
00:50:24,810 --> 00:50:29,830
So here's an example of a switch statement that is of nothing.

566
00:50:29,850 --> 00:50:37,850
Nothing about this is useful except to demonstrate some principles of how to turn a switch statement into assembly code.

567
00:50:37,850 --> 00:50:45,850
So the point of it is, as you know and see, you switch on a particular value, some type of integer value.

568
00:50:45,850 --> 00:50:53,850
And then it will match different cases where each of these cases has to be some constant numeric value.

569
00:50:53,850 --> 00:51:00,700
Constant integer value. And one of the weirdest features of the weirdest features of

570
00:51:00,900 --> 00:51:06,440
is, if you don't, at the end of a case, if you don't explicitly say break,

571
00:51:07,180 --> 00:51:09,280
it'll do what's known as falling through.

572
00:51:10,040 --> 00:51:19,300
So in this example here, it will divide Y by Z, and then it will just glide right into this statement

573
00:51:19,300 --> 00:51:20,740
that adds Z to W.

574
00:51:22,090 --> 00:51:27,450
This is, by the way, like one of the worst design decisions in the history of programming languages.

575
00:51:28,290 --> 00:51:31,230
And it's been the cause of more and more.

576
00:51:31,350 --> 00:51:37,350
you know, heartbreak and bugs that nobody could understand than any other single part of the language.

577
00:51:37,350 --> 00:51:43,710
And for some bizarre reason, they've kept it alive, even into Java and all these other languages,

578
00:51:43,710 --> 00:51:50,610
that fixed a lot of the worst parts, you know, the most dangerous parts of C, but somehow they kept this feature.

579
00:51:51,530 --> 00:52:00,930
And so just as a advice, as a programmer, if you ever, ever use this where you purposely want it to go from one case to other,

580
00:52:00,930 --> 00:52:04,170
you should have to put a comment in there to say,

581
00:52:04,990 --> 00:52:07,350
trust me, I really did want it to fall through.

582
00:52:07,530 --> 00:52:10,770
I know you almost never want to do this, but I do here.

583
00:52:12,040 --> 00:52:14,740
So that's a fairly common convention.

584
00:52:14,740 --> 00:52:20,600
And I don't know how many times I've had bugs that were because I forgot to put breaks in.

585
00:52:22,210 --> 00:52:25,030
Anyways, enough of my editorializing.

586
00:52:26,470 --> 00:52:32,130
The other feature you see and see is you can sort of bunch up.

587
00:52:32,150 --> 00:52:37,390
multiple cases, if you think of it, that's just a special case of the fall through, right?

588
00:52:37,450 --> 00:52:40,950
The case five has nothing to do but to fall through to six.

589
00:52:41,290 --> 00:52:43,590
But, so that's the same general way.

590
00:52:43,590 --> 00:52:47,910
And there's nothing, that's not, that's not bad, and it doesn't require a comment.

591
00:52:49,220 --> 00:52:51,320
And the other one is that there's a default.

592
00:52:51,640 --> 00:52:57,440
So if none of these cases match, either because X was zero or smaller, or X was a really

593
00:52:57,440 --> 00:53:03,630
big number, or in this case, if X were four, there's no matching case.

594
00:53:03,650 --> 00:53:08,210
And of course, you also know that these cases don't have to be in any particular order either.

595
00:53:09,420 --> 00:53:16,010
So that's all the sort of features of a case statement, a switch statement, sorry.

596
00:53:18,320 --> 00:53:22,620
So normally, if you write this in sort of, if you were told,

597
00:53:23,620 --> 00:53:32,360
thou shall not use switch statements anymore, what you'd probably do is write this big long chain of if else, if else, if else, else, right?

598
00:53:34,480 --> 00:53:36,100
And you'd expect that to be the way.

599
00:53:36,120 --> 00:53:37,820
the machine code, but it's not.

600
00:53:37,820 --> 00:53:42,540
And so let me show you what the machine code does.

601
00:53:42,540 --> 00:53:44,530
And it's kind of a long story.

602
00:53:44,530 --> 00:53:54,770
So think of the general form of it as being some blocks of code, the entry points of which are labeled

603
00:53:54,770 --> 00:53:56,990
by these case values.

604
00:53:56,990 --> 00:54:01,950
And then the blocks, you know, string together in various different ways and do various things.

605
00:54:01,950 --> 00:54:07,770
What I'm going to do is compile a code for all of those blocks and store them away and

606
00:54:07,770 --> 00:54:14,770
store them away in some part of memory, load up memory to contain these code blocks.

607
00:54:14,770 --> 00:54:25,250
And then I'm going to build a table. And each entry of this table describes the starting location of one of these code blocks.

608
00:54:25,250 --> 00:54:37,280
And I'll put them in order of my case labels. If I have, you know, I want to span a scope from, well, let's assume zero, but typically that's not the case

609
00:54:37,280 --> 00:54:44,280
0 up to n minus 1, I'll have this table will have that many entries of addresses

610
00:54:44,280 --> 00:54:52,080
to tell me where these code blocks are located.

611
00:54:52,080 --> 00:54:57,080
And then I'm going to use this really cool instruction,

612
00:54:57,080 --> 00:55:04,330
which is sort of like, it's as if you, it's like, it's like array indexing.

613
00:55:04,330 --> 00:55:06,330
So if you think of array indexing.

614
00:55:06,330 --> 00:55:09,440
It means you think of array indexing, it means you can grab a value

615
00:55:09,460 --> 00:55:13,460
out of the middle of some, you know, array,

616
00:55:13,460 --> 00:55:19,290
some set of values without having to step through them one by one.

617
00:55:19,290 --> 00:55:28,640
And it's the same idea here that I will take my value and use that to figure out directly where I should

618
00:55:28,640 --> 00:55:35,950
jump to a block of code without having to step through a bunch of other conditions.

619
00:55:35,950 --> 00:55:38,950
So, and you can see the efficiency you gain from that.

620
00:55:38,950 --> 00:55:39,950
It's the difference between.

621
00:55:39,950 --> 00:55:43,950
sort of in one step, knowing exactly where you want to be,

622
00:55:43,950 --> 00:55:51,950
versus stepping through, you know, on average, and over two conditions to get to where I want to go.

623
00:55:54,030 --> 00:55:57,030
So let's look at this at the assembly code level.

624
00:55:57,030 --> 00:56:06,490
And it's a little bit of a long thing because we have to build some machinery around this.

625
00:56:06,490 --> 00:56:10,490
So here's my example. I've got three arguments.

626
00:56:10,490 --> 00:56:15,490
So just remember I told you RDI and RSI and RSI.

627
00:56:15,490 --> 00:56:17,490
for the first two arguments.

628
00:56:17,490 --> 00:56:21,350
The third argument is in RDX.

629
00:56:21,350 --> 00:56:25,540
And here's the beginning of the switch code.

630
00:56:25,540 --> 00:56:31,020
It's just making a copy of argument Z here for some reason.

631
00:56:31,020 --> 00:56:36,020
And then it's looking at comparing x to 6.

632
00:56:36,020 --> 00:56:40,240
And why is 6 is 6 an important number?

633
00:56:40,240 --> 00:56:44,240
Because you see 6 was the largest value of any of my cases.

634
00:56:44,240 --> 00:56:50,600
And now it's using a jump instruction

635
00:56:50,600 --> 00:56:57,600
to go to L8, what we'll find is that tells you what the default behavior should be.

636
00:56:57,600 --> 00:57:06,730
So it's sort of flushing away the default cases, the default cases, the cases that are either X is too small or X is too large.

637
00:57:06,730 --> 00:57:08,730
And I should mention there's a clever trick there.

638
00:57:08,730 --> 00:57:16,730
If you look at JA, it means jump above, that's the unsigned comparison.

639
00:57:16,730 --> 00:57:19,730
And what it's making use of is our

640
00:57:19,730 --> 00:57:24,760
our result that you remember that if a number is negative,

641
00:57:24,760 --> 00:57:29,760
if you think of it as an unsigned value, it becomes very large positive value.

642
00:57:29,760 --> 00:57:34,760
So by doing the JA, instead of a JG,

643
00:57:34,760 --> 00:57:38,790
right, jump greater than, I'm doing jump above,

644
00:57:38,790 --> 00:57:43,860
it's saying, I want to jump to jump to the default if either

645
00:57:43,860 --> 00:57:50,970
X is greater than 6,

646
00:57:50,970 --> 00:57:56,970
than 6, but also it will cause it to jump if x is less than 0.

647
00:57:56,970 --> 00:57:58,380
